<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avantivarman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned <a href="/years/855">855</a>–883</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Avantivarman was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> from 855 to 883 and founder of the Utpala dynasty, which replaced the Karkota line. His reign was remembered as a time of recovery: his engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyya">Suyya</a> drained marshes and regulated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a> to win new farmland, and he founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avantipur">Avantipura</a> with its temples. His court patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poets and critics such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anandavardhana">Anandavardhana</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Avantivarman was the grandson of Utpala, one of five brothers who had taken control of the Karkota throne. He was raised by Utpala's minister Sura. Kashmir had been badly affected by civil wars over the previous forty years, and in 855 Avantivarman took the throne, establishing the Utpala dynasty and ending Karkota rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Avantivarman made the engineer and architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyya">Suyya</a> his prime minister. He restored the economy, strengthened the administration and internal security, and curbed the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damaras">Damaras</a>, a turbulent class of rural aristocrats. Suyya cleared boulders from the Vitasta, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>, built high embankments to prevent landslides and changed the river's course so that it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, which made large neighbouring lands fit for cultivation. He also arranged a permanent distribution of water to each village according to its need. During this time the price of paddy fell from 36 to 20 dinars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a> compared Suyya's work to what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> accomplished in four incarnations. The town of Suyyapura, modern Sopore in Baramulla district, is named after him.</p><p>Avantivarman was a patron of the arts and founded the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avantipur">Avantipur</a> and Suyapur. He built temples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and Buddhist monasteries, among them the temples of Avantiswara and Avantiswami at Avantipur. The most notable scholar of his court was Anandhavardana, the author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhvanyaloka">Dhvanyaloka</a>, and the poets Ranakar and Ananvardhana were also there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Avantivarman died in 883. A civil war among several descendants of Utpala followed, and the minister Ratnavardhana at last secured the throne for Avantivarman's son Shankaravarman, whose reign of 885 to 902 is remembered for its spirited foreign policy. The Avantiswami Temple in Awantipora, Pulwama, survives from his building.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Avantivarman of Kashmir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 855 <b>Avantivarman</b> took the throne, founding the Utpala dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/karkota-dynasty">Karkota dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 855 <b>Avantivarman</b>, a grandson of a minister of the Karkota dynasty, took the throne of Kashmir with the support of powerful ministers, founding a new dynasty after a period of weak Karkota rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/utpala-dynasty">Utpala dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
